    "### Intern Review\n",
    "\n",
    "**1. Search Question & Objective**\n",
    "Which observable page characteristics (e.g., word count, content age, update recency) and keyword market factors (e.g., search volume, competition, CPC, search intent) are reliably associated with trailing 90-day average search ranking position across client domains?\n",
    "\n",
    "Rather than attempting to reverse-engineer Google's full ranking algorithm, this lane models observed search outcomes across published content to evaluate which signals actually correlate with better visibility.\n",
    "\n",
    "**2.Unit of Analysis**\n",
    "\n",
    "The unit of analysis is one published content page(content_id) with a positive measured average position with the trailing 90-day window, grouped hierarichally by client. It is neither a side-wide aggreagate, an individual search query, nor a time-series day obeservatiom. Unmeasured pages are filtered out to keep the target strictly grounded in real observed search data.\n",
    "\n",
    "**3.Output**\n",
    "\n",
    "They system outputs a continious score [predicting target_avg_position_90d](where a lower value indicates better search position, closer to rank 1), accompanied by grouped feature importance and partial dependency analyses.\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "**4. Action Taken from the Output**\n",
    "\n",
    "Content strategists and SEO leads will use this output as a prioritization and traige filter. When evaluation content refreshes, the model highlights pages whose performance significantly deviates from expected positionts given their topic and metadata. Instead of manually auditing thousands of URLs, strategists inspect a focused batch of comparable outliers to determine if intent alignment or depth needs remediation before launching controlled editorial A/B tests/\n",
    "\n",
    "**5. Cost of a Wrong Recommendation**\n",
    "\n",
    "- Editorial Resource Waste: A falose positive can cause content teams to waste hunderds of hours artifically expanding articles with low-value fluff.\n",
    "- Organic Performance Degradation: Modying well-ranking pages based on misleading signals can dilute topical authority, hurt user experience, drop page rank, and damaging client organic search revenue.\n",
    "- Misguided Strategy: Trust in automated SEO recommendations decreases when prescribing blanket changes across diverse client verticals\n",
    "\n",
    "**6. Why Machine Learning Over a Fixed Heuristic**\n",
    "\n",
    "Fixed editorial heuristics (e.g., \"every article should be at least 2,500 words\" or \"update posts every 60 days\") fail because SEO variables interact non-linearly:\n",
    "\n",
    "- Ranking signals vary drastically by content type and intent; word count matters differently for in-depth comparison guides than for concise informational posts.\n",
    "\n",
    "- A single cutoff cannot account for keyword competition or market intent.\n",
    "\n",
    "- A machine learning scoring model can isolate non-linear interactions across features, control for missingness (e.g., handling the 26.69% missing word counts via explicit indicator flags), and be evaluated using group-held-out validation to ensure findings generalize across distinct client sites rather than memorizing domain-specific authority.\n",
    "\n",
    "**7. Empirical Justification from Starter Data (Why 7 Weeks is Worth It)**\n",
    "\n",
    "Three concrete findings from the starter dataset validate this problem space for an extended project:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. Substantial Sample Breadth with Natural Variance: After filtering unmeasured entries, the dataset retains 28,795 measured pages across 31 distinct client groups. The observed target (target_avg_position_90d) spans from 0.1 to 245.0 with a median of 11.4 and an IQR of 6.7 to 22.9, providing enough statistical power and variance to model multi-variable feature relationships without severe sample scarcity.\n",
    "\n",
    "2. Clear Empirical Baseline to Beat: A fixed median baseline on an 8-client held-out validation split (6,771 pages) yields an MAE of 8.55 positions. This sets a clear, testable threshold: any viable regression model must beat a 7.70 MAE (a 10% improvement) on completely unseen client domains, preventing premature claims of predictive signal.\n",
    "\n",
    "3. Substantial Baseline Shifts Across Content Types: The data confirms that broad heuristics cannot treat content uniformly:\n",
    "\n",
    "    - Feedly articles (1,366 pages) achieve a median position of 6.4, despite having 0.0% keyword volume coverage.\n",
    "\n",
    "    - Comparison articles (697 pages) average a median position of 8.5 with 100% word count and volume coverage.\n",
    "\n",
    "    - Keyword articles dominate the dataset (26,732 pages, ~92.8%) with a noticeably worse median position of 12.0 and 28.8% missing word counts.\n",
    "\n"
